---
import ReviewLayout from "../../layouts/ReviewLayout.astro"
import SourceFile from "../../components/SourceFile.astro"
import PrefixDiagram from "../../components/review/PrefixDiagram.astro"
import MergeCheckDiagram from "../../components/review/MergeCheckDiagram.astro"
import { reviewHref } from "../../lib/nav"
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/review-source"

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "fetch", text: "1. Fetch" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "prepare", text: "2. Prepare" },
  { depth: 3, slug: "packing", text: "Which files, in which order" },
  { depth: 3, slug: "groups", text: "File groups" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "find", text: "3. Find" },
  { depth: 3, slug: "tools", text: "The six tools" },
  { depth: 3, slug: "harvest", text: "The harvest and the closing call" },
  { depth: 3, slug: "whole", text: "A whole review instead" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "merge-and-check", text: "4. Merge and 5. Check" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "report", text: "6. Report" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "records", text: "Records on disk" },
]
const verifyTail = excerpt("review-prompt.entity.ts", "export const VERIFY_TAIL", "].join")
---

<ReviewLayout
  title="The pipeline"
  description="Fetch, prepare, find, merge, check, report: what each stage reads, what it decides, and what it keeps on disk."
  current="pipeline"
  headings={headings}
>
  <h2 id="fetch">1. Fetch</h2>
  <p>
    The reviewer runs <code>gh</code> and <code>git</code> on the host's own shell (<code>HostShell</code>), with the
    host's environment, since <code>gh</code> reads its login there. Every command whose output is parsed is read
    whole: an output the shell had to cut fails, and is never parsed half.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The pull request</strong> (<code>Forge.pullRequest</code>): its files, commits and its author's permission.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The commit under review</strong>: the head by default; with <code>--at first</code>, the commit the pull
      request was first pushed at; or any commit by its sha. Any commit but the head is reviewed against where its
      history meets the base, with the description as it stood when the commit was made, since a later edit often lists
      the fixes made after it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The clone</strong> (<code>Checkout.fetch</code>): a bare mirror clone, kept between reviews in a cache
      folder outside every git repository. Nothing is checked out for the model: what it reads comes from the clone's
      objects at the commit, by tree entry and blob.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The diff</strong>: from where the commit left the base to the commit (<code>base...head</code>), whole.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The repository's rules</strong>: the instruction files along the changed paths (<code>AGENTS.md</code>,
      <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and the like), read at the <em>base</em>, so the pull request cannot rewrite the rules it is
      reviewed by. A text met twice (a link from one name to another) is kept once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The CI</strong>: every run for the commit started by a pull request, a push or a merge queue; each failed
      run's log digested (escape codes stripped, repeated failures counted, lines cut), the whole log kept beside the
      records.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="prepare">2. Prepare</h2>
  <p>
    Preparing asks no model: <code>efferent-review prepare</code> stops here and prints the result. Every decision in it
    is a policy, a plain function the host may replace (<a href={reviewHref("plugins") + "#policies"}>ReviewPolicies</a>).
  </p>
  <h3 id="packing">Which files, in which order</h3>
  <p>
    Lockfiles, files the base's <code>.gitattributes</code> marks generated or vendored, binary and deleted files are
    left out; each is still named in a line with its counts, and a lockfile's line says whether a manifest changed too.
    The rest is packed under a token budget (24,000 by default, <code>--budget</code>) in this order:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>manifests, CI files and small config, smallest first;</li>
    <li>the source, in git's order;</li>
    <li>the tests;</li>
    <li>large config;</li>
    <li>pages and docs, each group largest first.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A file the budget leaves out is listed at the end of the packed diff with why, and the model can read its diff
    with <code>readDiff</code> all the same.
  </p>
  <h3 id="groups">File groups</h3>
  <p>
    By default a review is <em>focused</em> (<code>--focus groups</code>): the changed code files are grouped by
    package, the nearest folder holding a <code>package.json</code>. A package within the bounds (6,000 tokens of diff,
    8 files) is one group; a larger one is cut into units of one stem in one folder (an adapter with its test) and
    filled in order. Source groups come first, then groups of tests alone, then, only with <code>--config-turn</code>,
    one group of config. Within each tier the riskiest group goes first: a group whose added lines touch shared state,
    concurrency, processes, files, the network or secrets. Docs get no turn; every file no group holds is listed with
    why. A pull request with no code at all is reviewed whole instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="find">3. Find</h2>
  <p>
    Each group gets one finder turn, one group after another. A group starts only while the find stage's ledger still
    has room for its first call and the reserve its closing call needs, so a turn that starts can always report what
    it found; the groups after that are listed as not reviewed, with why. Every request of every turn starts with the
    same prefix:
  </p>
  <PrefixDiagram />
  <p>
    The turn's own message, its <em>tail</em>, asks it to read each file under review whole, then what its changed lines
    call and are called by, asking for reads that do not depend on each other in one answer; to hold the description's
    claims against the code; and to call <code>reportFinding</code> the moment it sees a defect, before it reads on,
    since a turn can be stopped at any call and a defect it has not reported is lost. By default it reports every
    plausible defect, sure or not (<code>--finder candidates</code>): the checks settle each one.
  </p>

  <h3 id="tools">The six tools</h3>
  <p>
    Every turn, finder's or check's, is offered all six tools in one order (then any a host adds, see
    <a href={reviewHref("plugins") + "#host-tools"}>tools of your own</a>), so their declarations stay in the cached
    part of the request. They read the clone's objects at the commit under review, never a folder on disk, and each
    one bounds and pages its own answer, so no answer grows with a file.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Its limits</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>readLines</code></td><td>Numbered lines of a file at the commit, and the call that reads on.</td><td>150 lines a call, 30,000 characters a page, each line cut at 500; files of at most 1 MiB; never a folder, a link, a submodule or a binary file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>searchCode</code></td><td>Lines of tracked files that hold a text (<code>git grep</code>, fixed text unless <code>regex</code> is set), optionally under one path.</td><td>50 lines shown, each cut at 200 characters; a pattern of at most 300; a search too long or too big answered in fixed words.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>listFiles</code></td><td>Tracked files whose paths match a glob.</td><td>200 paths; a glob of at most 200 characters, matched by the reviewer's own code in linear time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>readDiff</code></td><td>The diff of any changed file, those the packed diff left out included.</td><td>Pages of 150 lines or 30,000 characters.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>reportFinding</code></td><td>Records a finding under its turn; a failed result in a check.</td><td>A file the pull request changes, at most 40 lines; the claim kept to 1,500 characters, the evidence to its first whole lines within 1,000.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>verdict</code></td><td>Records a check's verdict: confirmed, refuted or unsure, a reason and a quote; a failed result in a finder turn.</td><td>The first verdict stands. Reason 1,500 characters, quote 1,000.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A refusal is a failed result in words that say what to do instead (a path the pull request does not change names
    the files it does), so the model can try again in its next step. A git command that fails is answered in fixed
    words, never git's own, which may name a path on the host.
  </p>

  <h3 id="harvest">The harvest and the closing call</h3>
  <p>
    A group's turn is capped at 24 steps; the harvest and the closing call come after the cap. When it answers in
    words, it is asked once more: the host's note asks
    it to report each possible defect it noticed or weighed and did not report, also one it judged too small or was
    not sure of; only that answer's <code>reportFinding</code> calls run. A turn that reaches its step cap gets a
    closing call instead, which may only report. Both are on
    <a href={reviewHref("loop") + "#closing"}>the loop's page</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="whole">A whole review instead</h3>
  <p>
    With <code>--focus whole</code> every turn reads the whole packed diff: <code>--turns</code> turns (one by
    default), each walking the changed files in an order of its own, dealt like cards, and capped at
    <code>--max-steps</code> (16). The first turn runs alone until its first model call returns, having written the
    provider's cache of the shared prefix; then the others start beside it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="merge-and-check">4. Merge and 5. Check</h2>
  <MergeCheckDiagram />
  <p>
    Checks run in rounds: first each kept bucket's best finding, the most severe, then the one with the longest
    evidence the file's diff holds. A later round checks a bucket's next finding only while no check has confirmed the
    bucket, up to three a bucket, and up to three checks at once. Each check is a turn of its own, capped at 12 calls,
    on the prefix of the group holding its finding's file, and its tail asks it to refute first:
  </p>
  <SourceFile file="review-prompt.entity.ts" code={verifyTail} />
  <p>
    A confirmed verdict counts only when its quote is in the head's file near the cited lines: held by those lines,
    with a few lines of slack, whitespace and a tool's line numbers taken off, and with at least one quoted line a whole
    line of the cited lines that is long enough and holds a name. A brace or a bare <code>return</code> proves nothing.
    A confirmed verdict whose quote fails counts as unsure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">6. Report</h2>
  <p>
    Only confirmed findings are reported, each bucket once. At most six are shown, sorted by severity, high first,
    then by path and line, each with its claim, its quoted evidence and the check's reason. Then, apart, the findings no
    check settled (unsure, or confirmed with a quote that was not found), and each kept bucket no check gave a verdict
    on, with why. With nothing confirmed the report says so: <code>No findings.</code>, or
    <code>No confirmed findings.</code> when some are unsettled, or <code>Incomplete: no turn finished.</code> when no
    turn answered or reached its step cap. It also lists each turn's summary or why it is incomplete, each group with
    its turn or why it was not reviewed, and what each stage's calls used.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report is printed, or written as JSON with <code>--json</code>. Nothing is posted to the pull request.
  </p>

  <h2 id="records">Records on disk</h2>
  <p>
    Each run keeps everything it was given and everything it did, for offline scoring, in a folder of its own. Every
    file is written whole or not at all (a temporary file renamed over it), and nothing cleans them up.
  </p>
  <pre class="rv-tree not-prose">~/.efferent-review/                       <span class="c">the cache root (--cache), outside every git repository</span>
  &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/mirror.git              <span class="c">the bare clone</span>
  &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/clones/ packages/ …     <span class="c">what the sandboxes use: throwaway clones, the bot's package cache,</span>
                                          <span class="c">verdicts on the base, node_modules snapshots, scratch</span>
  records/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/                 <span class="c">the records root (--records)</span>
    &lt;number&gt;/&lt;sha&gt;/                       <span class="c">the commit under review, its full sha</span>
      ci-&lt;run id&gt;.log                     <span class="c">each failed CI run's log, whole</span>
      &lt;run&gt;/                              <span class="c">one run: when it started, and six random hex digits</span>
        prepared.json                     <span class="c">the prepared review</span>
        turn-1/ turn-2/ …                 <span class="c">each finder turn's journal</span>
        check-1/ check-2/ …               <span class="c">each check's journal</span>
        review.json                       <span class="c">kept as it runs (complete: false), replaced as each stage ends</span></pre>
  <p>
    <code>review.json</code> holds the prompt's and the tool set's versions, every finding with its turn, every
    bucket with its votes and why it was kept, every check with the finding it was shown, its verdict and whether its
    quote held, and each call's tokens and cost.
  </p>
</ReviewLayout>
